#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c7d7c2-430d-5af8-9d05-fa5665e62862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424149} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2a26b3-3177-579e-877d-0fe20ffae536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424158} {:answer/id #uuid "9406cdae-72e2-5c66-8ad2-f1e16969ae1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424162} {:answer/id #uuid "af88273c-a488-5b6d-af48-680f8f2c6cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424163} {:answer/id #uuid "de9aadb1-15c7-5202-b107-ef067be5cce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424149} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d447ea2-8560-5077-b8b9-f3304d7a91d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03a9f960-3754-59ca-a7ce-a0ebf6a90fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-39294" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=v+w,\\,y=u+w,\\,z=u+v.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)(0-1)+(1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146610e3-5c1c-5561-9ca3-9774666c6c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-a-b-c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424150} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fd2ac-fcea-50a9-92dd-45cccffc9e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-abc" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424155} {:answer/id #uuid "5938eea0-a7c5-5a7f-bf1f-a9fd0efaaf3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a+b+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424157} {:answer/id #uuid "7a38c64c-896b-5232-a5e4-465265d40434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "abc" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424161} {:answer/id #uuid "b5179334-ac94-5f42-8f5c-8c9ece45c7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424161} :answer-field/id #uuid "3708f8e2-7d98-51bd-8b21-20ee27c9855a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0fc3f590-a25e-51e5-80e8-0d05b569e022" :question/math-academy-id "q-39664" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=au,\\,y=bv,\\,z=cw.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $a$, $b$ and $c$ are fixed constants." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =a & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =b & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}a & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}b & 0 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(bc-0) \\\\  & =abc.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e37282e-8869-5ad2-9f01-e062a7d21846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v=2{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424153} {:answer/id #uuid "4a31e95b-7336-5699-a485-0b22a7563452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424154} {:answer/id #uuid "56201c08-96c0-58d8-9ed7-c9a2a6ecea11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424156} {:answer/id #uuid "7447254c-742a-5c7f-adb1-193a3168832a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424159} {:answer/id #uuid "7473d403-04c8-5521-92cd-20ba6d10f904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424154} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a37030-5cd9-5e43-a291-e19dfc94c67f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b50230b8-d517-560a-9ede-747bedde3c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161811" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u}+v \\\\ v+w \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u}+v,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(v-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{u}(v-w) & =0 \\\\ v-w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-w=0$ defines the plane $v=w.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba467583-3a17-5baa-8344-2ff452a1d080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424165} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7a65b8-5911-5995-890e-26eb26de2d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{vw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424166} {:answer/id #uuid "cda476b6-e024-5c13-a813-a0217e4446ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424167} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf34300-0330-5f0e-9e12-24a74c4b83db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2u}{v-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424170} {:answer/id #uuid "fa885cf6-9f29-528c-8aeb-86e960fc0083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{w}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424165} :answer-field/id #uuid "f602c505-c9b2-58a6-873a-3101dc02439a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bbf87425-cede-5e7e-870f-278bd2b11904" :question/math-academy-id "q-161808" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ y \\\\ xz\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{y},\\,v(x,y,z)=y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =z\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =z(0-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{z}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z}{y} & =-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0584ff70-04f9-53f4-b918-d40c95661794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424148} {:answer/id #uuid "274585a0-b8c0-5396-9ba1-f844d74ca493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424151} {:answer/id #uuid "2dad3fa0-8b4c-595d-a450-31167f619d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424152} {:answer/id #uuid "d83940ba-986b-5a11-adf3-d93e8cc8e5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424168} {:answer/id #uuid "f587a66a-7aeb-56f0-8880-52bcb09867b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9faf9fb-252c-510a-b14d-4675e0404650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fa96e0b4-9142-5b9e-b173-68571a2c2de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161797" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=2y,\\,w=3z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(6-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afc3cbd-80cd-5f79-95d1-b02b58d14af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ce/ce7690cdb4a4da4841dc7f551b73d379945141964de4f9de00dbe6a39a6e36e8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424184} {:answer/id #uuid "25ed12c7-2e43-5b16-b2e3-173b3c5367b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e2/e239433cc1c62333506d14eac022488f2c6786837f17f20e6a1fbba9f25dbf12.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424185} {:answer/id #uuid "99f6e268-33a9-5e9a-a609-e6ee2c4c38d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f6/f6264905bceebe59d764a88f8566dc5eb21237dd28ae5c2052fbd3c766ba6c14.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424186} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dba748-9027-5a4a-bf33-0feb67920158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4c/4c5c4b297d1aaaa672628f0621e8ed71fbacedd9f418d7d7068da8cba5847315.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424187} {:answer/id #uuid "cad5833b-0aa5-5948-b9b9-b322bb2fcf7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/96/96851fd456d3b9f0a6af941a255926f35e29bbe778ed05556af3f69145e38ef2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424188} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f1a53d-9e00-54ab-b914-f5c8b1d4efa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d9aa2a3-1ea1-5a5e-9577-ca5789b6206d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122737" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =2(x-y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2}) \\\\  & =-2(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2(x-y)\\,i-2(x-y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(1,2),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =2\\cdot (2-1)\\,i-2\\cdot (2-1)\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,2) & =2\\cdot (1-2)\\,i-2\\cdot (1-2)\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,i-2\\cdot (1-(-1))\\,j=4\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/96/96851fd456d3b9f0a6af941a255926f35e29bbe778ed05556af3f69145e38ef2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b359e4-02cd-5e97-8b05-35ed5f867cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424602} {:answer/id #uuid "65eebd17-cd71-544b-a441-a52714eabfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424606} {:answer/id #uuid "97231ff5-005f-5e53-bec5-aa0b22f5882f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424610} {:answer/id #uuid "e35dbb22-7632-5914-b08b-f1a1a963fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424614} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b66872-c5ce-599c-8fa3-26dd57b225b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424615} :answer-field/id #uuid "d716774a-ff3b-5a8f-950b-7d77b8284d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37832b23-49d2-5d4e-9f43-b03259f479e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77145" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3c874f3c4564795a2ddcfb4c2896c1bc0ef1b4b7b3d8e8318f42d15643942d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $UVWU,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b97760-1703-5852-afc4-69acd21e7cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424603} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd6afbc-8569-51f5-a569-dd388d648b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} {:answer/id #uuid "79d50006-6f06-56c3-bce2-b65f3e1eb21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424607} {:answer/id #uuid "d37fe04f-4a91-5f51-9524-f7477c2a7f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424612} {:answer/id #uuid "e17522ae-4622-53a9-bb60-7bec4bbcce91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdba31b-0f4f-583e-a52e-f0510ad8e3c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "515517e3-98f2-5a9f-ba03-8d3b47263ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-77258" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the section of the curve $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i-\\sqrt{t}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 4.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-\\sqrt{t}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j) \\\\  & =t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2})\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{4}^{3/2}}{3}-0)+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d6b3-ad5e-5dc6-8ebb-24366de741cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424604} {:answer/id #uuid "90b2b589-9e4a-50b5-aade-f9285dbfd609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}2{e}^{x+y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424608} {:answer/id #uuid "91f4495b-123f-57a5-9de2-b6a7f1714f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424609} {:answer/id #uuid "adc05a61-f7ae-5052-9af4-0da99b3ef9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{2(x+y+z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424611} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa1700d-0795-5ad2-aa9c-b22297bf2101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424604} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef1db59-b0fd-505e-b25a-7a9041ecb1ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7d70870-3ade-5c38-9cda-96260cf64575" :question/math-academy-id "q-125414" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)={e}^{y+z}\\,i+{e}^{x+z}\\,j+{e}^{x+y}\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11019ac0-2397-5082-8da2-e2cda61c67f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431149} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca8c25f-0afe-549b-bd9e-0579f77dfd35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431152} {:answer/id #uuid "617b3f2e-e124-52d6-b054-729c57763e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431157} {:answer/id #uuid "8493f5bf-0293-5a89-b43a-eae5ba48387d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431159} {:answer/id #uuid "e69380fc-03e3-562e-97ec-2bf770b22d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431152} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84d6437-9ec9-5b4b-a3b3-8f14a689f608" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1d416162-8b5b-53c3-aa15-9f678fcebfbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-162229" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+0\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f86a33-c575-56fe-bdb7-9730885ac9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431147} {:answer/id #uuid "20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431151} {:answer/id #uuid "54da2db0-1ce1-5f93-af27-c7663a8c8354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431155} {:answer/id #uuid "c8614eac-4ce9-50b5-a743-5b04a0329efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431170} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3f8a13-dd60-5d9d-81be-3575b6037de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431151} :answer-field/id #uuid "257fc3b0-4eee-568e-ab48-ba72ee13cf19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f7d4767-ad54-585a-81f3-9a4a8f681379" :question/math-academy-id "q-162373" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x+y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-x+y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-2(x-1)^{2}-2(y+1)^{2}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot 2(x-1)-(-x+y)\\cdot 2(y+1)-2((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)(1-x)+(x-y)(y+1)-(x-1)^{2}-(y+1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4(-{r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{r}^{3}+2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-{r}^{3}\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi {r}^{3}-2\\pi r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8\\pi {[-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\pi (-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431185 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5ee6b0-2be5-5581-a028-5db7075b5455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431148} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceea66b-fcc6-5f70-83a1-36dc6fc02ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431161} {:answer/id #uuid "9df165e3-93dc-582f-b6c7-50fa8041ebef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431162} {:answer/id #uuid "b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431168} {:answer/id #uuid "f29e0799-e667-5d4c-b0bf-49fdbd64159a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431168} :answer-field/id #uuid "e70a93bf-0c70-586e-8362-c72402361cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "54c148d3-85ed-598f-9fe2-1c4177e8c1c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162999" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2z\\,i+3y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,z={t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+3y\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =(-3{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}+\\frac{13}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-3+\\frac{13}{4}-\\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3927b4fa-c581-50e7-9636-601f0c7f2db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431154} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c0ebdd-99e9-5a8d-ba80-6dbf22ca7b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431163} {:answer/id #uuid "ad732c00-dd52-57e7-a26e-fd44e8ff888d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431164} {:answer/id #uuid "b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431167} {:answer/id #uuid "e08a22bc-8ede-59c0-a3f4-3fa119eeb7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431167} :answer-field/id #uuid "db93ea83-d9f2-5f0b-8dff-03ea91428db7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "60b9502a-1750-5d1a-ac6a-f7d7a17af2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162314" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=t+{t}^{2},\\,z=-t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{t}^{2})\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (1+2t)+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\cdot (-1-2t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}+{t}^{4}+\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{2}{5}+1-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431150} {:answer/id #uuid "3859679f-08da-5297-b35b-fcbd0703da61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431153} {:answer/id #uuid "83932af9-e25e-5cce-b094-ca4fbc00e243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431158} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb9b526-feac-52a5-810d-3cdfe908fcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431166} {:answer/id #uuid "da95a228-ca5c-59a2-aafd-0aafd2625911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431150} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3c5fce6-36d3-5f8a-948a-98bad40e6ffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9aea1d19-f86f-5208-99cb-c76a6a49c45e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162364" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $y=1+x,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{0}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab155520a21d38483e8b0978a04972e1a96c8d273d23ca7aa36bb1846189b4c8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58c1fd16-ca29-5709-a267-219424eaaabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431156} {:answer/id #uuid "8c03109d-b971-53ee-a86a-7e35ed350bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431160} {:answer/id #uuid "adea9c1e-27a3-5208-9dd6-795f2fa57e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431165} {:answer/id #uuid "b705117a-dca8-5892-b76c-7de8a8a5594d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431169} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fa4475-9d5e-511a-8566-852d47df5af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431169} :answer-field/id #uuid "42e70451-3a35-5ee4-bde4-603fef9a0a12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f4551bd-d0a1-5978-99b9-17e8a12314a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162228" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=z\\,j-y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,j-y\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (2t-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t))\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(2\\pi t)-2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi )\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\pi [t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\pi (1-0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81ae3e78-b1f4-5477-8bac-c412eb3c2747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431280} :answer-field/id #uuid "0902a00b-6972-5278-913c-8072ffb99155" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5473d4ab-39f5-5e1e-ade8-0e74627b89e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-349299" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=4 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 4x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0)+4(0-1) \\\\  & =2\\pi -4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dcd62ed-d1d5-5d66-b9a7-d3752b72ffcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434263} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f59ee40-6a49-5f76-9b09-b56f1e7b8c65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb3f03d-cfef-5f9f-b396-26b221d4f856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434266} :answer-field/id #uuid "62dc0b83-a470-59d2-b62b-b5ccbaa907c1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c46acbe-8af8-5805-a582-9ade9300c9a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-279199" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{13}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=13$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=13,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{13}=2\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "224b79cf-bf9d-546e-92e5-652da96853b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434264} {:answer/id #uuid "239820f5-492c-5074-bb16-a6af5ed54f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434265} {:answer/id #uuid "53b5a01d-de71-5112-abcb-3f57176855d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434267} {:answer/id #uuid "64c8fe0e-8e01-5ebd-869d-3e05d36b78cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434269} {:answer/id #uuid "9428f7fa-4636-5ad9-ac5c-92e2a7fbe6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434270} :answer-field/id #uuid "29540716-87b2-5957-a8c4-7141164fdefb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5fbef9b7-68b9-5b6d-beff-b38a63574b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16458" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2adb84964a605495c84921943ec76f9820ba18851cda817037dd6022808d1fd7.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb06d992-e82f-5977-a714-2f2fd87616b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434271} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f8d6b7-9861-51ad-96d2-5cd3b96789b2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434272} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56513915-f193-5d63-936d-07a8b133ceae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434268} :answer-field/id #uuid "878be2a7-5abd-5dc4-9fe0-30ce370e3435" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb8398f9-e97a-58eb-bd8b-b49ccd2d689d" :question/math-academy-id "q-279167" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b1374a-677b-550b-816a-6cbd02c5f061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434818} {:answer/id #uuid "29ead56c-2949-5fb0-8895-cdd6a87df4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434820} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3bf5e7-fb1f-5845-863a-15bfb2564e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434824} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e03b7e-e933-5e07-a534-f79399c89baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434825} {:answer/id #uuid "c757d8e1-3c0b-5fc9-9360-e929a186b1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434820} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1855324-ee18-529c-ba15-53c93565cb17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22bda1b7-731c-5be9-8f1b-6668d9e068b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161253" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,xz,\\,yz⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{u}^{2},\\,v⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}auv+b{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,xz,\\,yz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=v} \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,uv,\\,{u}^{2}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨v,\\,uv,\\,{u}^{2}v⟩\\cdot ⟨2u,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =v\\cdot 2u+uv\\cdot (-1)+{u}^{2}v\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2uv-uv+0 \\\\  & =uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot uv+0\\cdot {u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+0=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4ed277-51d3-5190-9f15-e73dd884cf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434819} {:answer/id #uuid "471dabbc-d133-52dc-b559-394801c709a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434821} {:answer/id #uuid "777af6b2-9aef-5099-aac7-98b1ec37120e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434822} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1f231c-84b6-5409-b4c5-4c9196b18b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434823} {:answer/id #uuid "de47629f-21da-52b1-941b-ae2f3625cecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434819} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1a0c12-6b57-5589-b4e1-416cdc457a68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5815497-10bb-5d31-b1ef-c78b6eb79a17" :question/math-academy-id "q-161259" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 0,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-u-v,\\,v-u,\\,2⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-u-v,\\,v-u,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨x,\\,y,\\,-z⟩|}_{x=u+v\\,y=v-u\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,-uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,-uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-u-v,\\,v-u,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)\\cdot (-u-v)+(v-u)\\cdot (v-u)+(-uv)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}-2uv-{v}^{2}+{v}^{2}-2uv+{u}^{2}-2uv \\\\  & =-6uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{-2}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\cdot [\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40aa43f3-5052-52eb-9041-2ff9b581bcb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436086} {:answer/id #uuid "4b176e8d-dda0-56b4-82b1-b56ebe13e094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436089} {:answer/id #uuid "c65ea214-6d95-58fa-a971-d2654e7a972b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436107} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fb3b67-eae3-54a3-a2f2-8864cb03f007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436111} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc96a06-c359-5bf6-a8b3-a2cef416b538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436114}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436086} :answer-field/id #uuid "050f7890-b08d-5d96-a6f3-aa1024a4d86d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15e24e7e-a45b-5130-9771-640280afca4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74953" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{1+(-1)}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+z)+y(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}x+y & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e5ed22-82cd-5dd4-ae2b-e1265c44598a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436076} {:answer/id #uuid "15697aa4-8790-5d84-b361-1fcd5c853cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436079} {:answer/id #uuid "3a28c3c9-2e36-51fc-96e3-067a7a2ef398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436083} {:answer/id #uuid "40144898-c856-5d2a-8c25-56d7a6e1fd50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436085} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3eb38d-a93e-535b-a33a-1db8c0313fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436085} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b9cba1-39d8-5078-b34a-2ad5eaf58ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6e200150-12cc-5141-a4cc-a34826db19ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-74962" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{3+(-3)+4}-2}=\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{4}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y+4-4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{z(x+y)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2) & =2(\\sqrt{3-3+4}+2) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}+2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "416f53e7-0424-5030-ac7c-3c49b5372569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436087} {:answer/id #uuid "5aceb8dd-e06d-5296-8078-5ae4cc750061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436093} {:answer/id #uuid "696c1dec-c738-5e33-a673-9e3ab3c19a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436095} {:answer/id #uuid "83965ef5-938e-5708-bac6-916fb84faf46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436098} {:answer/id #uuid "b61fdeeb-ac42-57e7-8628-5828106b4cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436105}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436087} :answer-field/id #uuid "65295b29-6d49-5c32-a7c7-3761c22ba9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e76204f-7492-5638-91de-b17ed7af74ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-129323" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y} & =\\frac{2(2\\cdot 1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d1791-5b87-5fc0-88f4-c35a94cf6f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436075} {:answer/id #uuid "566a1241-e2de-561e-9daf-82cc2652adff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436092} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0718a8-d111-53b4-8167-8361933440ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436094} {:answer/id #uuid "705005f1-4e76-51ab-bd86-bbc4756b8e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436096} {:answer/id #uuid "8579266b-a01b-54d9-9032-9c94a818a557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436094} :answer-field/id #uuid "c964af35-b530-511c-ba6c-a0a70d2a6c2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88f91bc6-5d92-5d89-9639-1db1ee91906d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74908" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the expression under the radical   is negative. However, for the point $(-2,-2)$ the product $x\\cdot y$ is not negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot (-2)}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0924a936-e922-5c55-aa02-d684eeb3f2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436077} {:answer/id #uuid "753869fe-932f-5db8-83a0-37c1015d805e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436097} {:answer/id #uuid "90465371-f2d3-501a-b599-455e14d6af8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436100} {:answer/id #uuid "ae730908-c126-5126-80c5-7ba1a6367fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436104} {:answer/id #uuid "f1888faf-1d96-547d-a447-76ace2c376d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436113} :answer-field/id #uuid "78bd852f-26bb-5b2b-90e2-b2dc4782250d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9df4444a-990b-5f68-bec9-ae4f2c45450b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129402" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{({0}^{2})(0)}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}(0)}{{x}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a55cd8-59d4-51e6-bfc3-6f7e89d540fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436078} {:answer/id #uuid "289a71e6-833a-5782-99bf-9eaaf974a1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436082} {:answer/id #uuid "924475dc-6d5b-55d7-b419-2671e1182f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436101} {:answer/id #uuid "baaec9e1-8b5e-5f7f-8ef8-59b96e26a955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436106} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a58b3c-76e3-54b6-ab35-4bf1be82b8db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436106} :answer-field/id #uuid "8116b6c2-1cab-5c9a-af1a-53984a742595" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a678f4db-f59f-58b6-9d8f-ba8fadd30c17" :question/math-academy-id "q-129336" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2(1)^{2}(-1)+1(-1)^{2}}{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2})}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)(x+y)}{x+y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{x+y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y) & =1(1-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e050b6b-4c71-5b1d-95f2-c237914af9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436080} {:answer/id #uuid "23db4813-aed1-5501-99cf-12aa83f3383d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436081} {:answer/id #uuid "4c47b885-90cb-59cf-b936-3007bf1a5ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436090} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f9219d-acd8-5505-9300-47d9ac52607b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436102} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f4f9e0-fb6f-53dd-a9eb-91c9e707abd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436081} :answer-field/id #uuid "f486c765-ecd7-5a87-8f26-635e2f270bec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c11b4e02-4fa9-528c-867b-1ff09ac00c10" :question/math-academy-id "q-74911" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(4,-4,-2)$ does not cause the  denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(4,-4,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4-(-4)}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{8}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da1bffe-a256-5463-b543-084468d49c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436084} {:answer/id #uuid "47a24a05-5058-5973-b9b9-1f6b550f6f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436088} {:answer/id #uuid "5512019f-2424-5d94-8ce3-00141bd0391c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436091} {:answer/id #uuid "aa885501-8121-5a91-81dd-6ab6a0717534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436103} {:answer/id #uuid "d4dd68e7-ba4b-5f9e-bb24-90eb7a550447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436108} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebddb13-5c72-52ae-80f4-9d372c2249ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cdbbe1e3-c4b9-56df-b019-b8be69ef537e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74959" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6+3}-3}{6+3-{3}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y}-x)(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y})^{2}-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{6+y-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cedb0ea-68aa-5297-8739-1c67694130cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436545} {:answer/id #uuid "89c32784-67a9-5a3f-9541-f6b61f434ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436554} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f43775-fb77-555a-84a3-313713b28808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436575} {:answer/id #uuid "cef87b38-87f4-5c33-ae55-63daa3d3acae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436577} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0a7424-1567-5720-902e-f99517a66dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436545} :answer-field/id #uuid "4182c953-888a-5a78-b08a-c5df63ce2717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d55bb11-970c-5f1f-831a-44fb97e659c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132261" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(0,\\sqrt{\\pi }).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (3x\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\cdot (3x\\cdot 0-2y) \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,\\sqrt{\\pi }):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(0,\\sqrt{\\pi })} & =4(\\sqrt{\\pi })\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(0)-{(\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(0)-{(\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
